  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...While seas have decreased considerably over the\nlast 24 hours, there is still some potential for minor ocean\noverwash at the next high tide this evening (around 8 PM) for\nportions of the Outer Banks. In addition, minor soundside \nflooding (1 ft or less) is still possible for coastal areas due \nto already elevated waters levels and high astronomical tides. CF\nAdvisories continue through late tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An upper level ridge is forecast to build over \nthe Southeast U.S. later this week, but guidance has slowed down\nsome on when this occurs. In light of this, the warmest \ntemperatures of the week have trended towards the end of the \nweek (Thursday/Friday). Beneath the ridge, warming low-level \nthicknesses and persistent southerly flow may allow inland highs\nto get back close to 90 degrees (roughly 10 degrees above \nnormal as we head into the first few days of October). The \nhumidity is forecast to steadily return as well, but with \nridging in place, the chance of showers and thunderstorms looks \nto stay low for much of the week. The next front will approach\nthe area next weekend.",
